I've written and committed the plan for this session (6c5226b0): two task files and one issue-response file. session_plan/ is gitignored, so the plain git add in the instructions refused the files. I used git add -f, which looks like how assessment.md got committed before.

Task 1 (product, self-driven): a numeric flag with a value that isn't a number exits 2 at startup.
• The problem: today --max-tokens abc, --temperature hot, --max-turns x and --context-window big print a warning, make a paid call on the default value, and exit 0. The assessment confirmed this on the current build. Only someone who made a typo hits this path, so nobody chose that outcome.
• The fix: reuse the exit-2 refusal that an unknown --provider already gets, before any request is sent. The logic and tests go in src/cli_config.rs, because src/cli.rs is 5 lines from the size limit that fails the test suite.
• The test: a check on the built binary that it exits 2 and never attempts the call.
• Config files keep the warning: a project's .yoyo.toml may be written by someone else, so refusing would block everyone who uses that repo over one person's typo.
• Left for later: --thinking ultra, unknown flags, out-of-range numbers and the config-file decision go into one new issue, filed before the push.

Task 2 (product, closes an issue): Fixes #1023.
• The backlog has 18 open issues (over the limit of 12), so this slot has to close one. #1023 has the least work left: items 1 and 2 are already done.
• The last item is real. I checked yoagent 0.25.16: list_files adds find's warning lines after its file-count line. The deny-list filter keeps everything after that line, so a warning like find: '<denied path>': Permission denied can show the model the name of a path it isn't allowed to see. The contents don't leak, only the name.
• The task filters those warning lines with the same deny check, and drops the warning heading if every line under it is removed.
• It also checks whether the "Listing error" result (returned when find lists nothing) bypasses the filter, and tests the untested --allow-dir case.
• Anything new it finds gets its own issue before the push.

